<h2>4. Presentation Q&A Cheatsheet</h2>

<div class="quote-box">
  <strong>Q: Why didn't you use ChatGPT or an LLM for department triage?</strong><br>
  <em>Answer:</em> "LLMs introduce 2-to-3-second network latency, cost money per API call, and can hallucinate. Our TF-IDF + Naive Bayes pipeline runs 100% offline, costs zero dollars, executes in under 1 millisecond on CPU, and is mathematically explainable."
</div>

<div class="quote-box">
  <strong>Q: Why DBSCAN instead of K-Means for hotspot clustering?</strong><br>
  <em>Answer:</em> "K-Means requires specifying the number of clusters $k$ beforehand, which is impossible in a dynamic city. DBSCAN discovers the exact number of dense clusters automatically and ignores isolated noise."
</div>

<div class="quote-box">
  <strong>Q: Why did you pick SQLite instead of MongoDB or PostgreSQL?</strong><br>
  <em>Answer:</em> "SQLite is zero-configuration and runs self-contained from a single file, eliminating server setup hurdles for demonstrations. Because we use SQLAlchemy 2.0 ORM, the codebase is database-agnostic—we can switch to PostgreSQL simply by updating one configuration string."
</div>

<div class="quote-box">
  <strong>Q: How does the system ensure privacy between citizens?</strong><br>
  <em>Answer:</em> "We implemented strict role-based access control and a dedicated endpoint (<code>/api/complaints/my</code>). The JWT token extracts the citizen's ID, and the query enforces <code>WHERE citizen_id = current_user.id</code>, guaranteeing no citizen can view another citizen's complaints."
</div>

